  "id": "wsuzch2",
  "title": "Chapter 9: Chemical Reactions",
  "order": 58,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ildmkq4",
      "content": "Chemical reactions are processes in which substances undergo chemical changes to form new substances with different properties. During a chemical reaction, atoms rearrange themselves to form new molecules. Chemical reactions are essential in everyday life, industrial processes and biological systems."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Governing Law",
          "value": "Conservation of Mass",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Primary Types",
          "value": "5 Core Classes",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Key Process",
          "value": "Redox Reactions",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Rate Modifier",
          "value": "Catalysts",
          "icon": "clock"
        }
      ],
      "id": "nx0xf6k"
    },
    {
      "id": "tc2imvy",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning of Chemical Reaction"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "A chemical reaction rearranges atoms by breaking existing bonds and forming new chemical bonds.",
        "Balanced chemical equations ensure the total number of atoms of each element remains identical on both sides.",
        "Oxidation involves gaining oxygen or losing hydrogen, whereas reduction involves losing oxygen or gaining hydrogen.",
        "Exothermic reactions release energy to the surroundings, while endothermic reactions absorb heat.",
        "Corrosion and rancidity are prominent real-world consequences of slow atmospheric oxidation."
      ],
      "id": "x8iiq5u"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "j12bcd7",
      "content": "• A chemical reaction is a process in which one or more substances are transformed into new substances."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cujrbwi",
      "content": "• The substances that take part in a reaction are called reactants."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "peeyibj",
      "content": "• The substances formed in a reaction are called products."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8yv0aqj",
      "content": "• Chemical reactions involve breaking and forming of chemical bonds."
    },
    {
      "id": "1y0o94m",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Chemical Equation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jotxo1b",
      "content": "A chemical equation is a symbolic representation of a chemical reaction using chemical formulas."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vo410ch",
      "content": "Example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "3jf1c9n",
      "content": "2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O"
    },
    {
      "id": "tk135md",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Balanced Chemical Equation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "llzwvvy",
      "content": "A balanced chemical equation has an equal number of atoms of each element on both sides."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jawdi81",
      "content": "Example:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "48bn2ex",
      "content": "CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O"
    },
    {
      "id": "3hyt1hw",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Indicators of Chemical Reaction"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "wt320gc",
      "content": "• Change in colour"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "i2jx0qo",
      "content": "• Change in temperature"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "khmrm1m",
      "content": "• Formation of gas"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "hb17seo",
      "content": "• Formation of precipitate"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bn8u60m",
      "content": "• Emission of light or sound"
    },
    {
      "id": "h0gzbwn",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Types of Chemical Reactions"
    },
    {
      "id": "mhs5817",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Reaction Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>General Form</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Example</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Combination Reaction</p>",
            "<p>A + B → AB</p>",
            "<p>2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Decomposition Reaction</p>",
            "<p>AB → A + B</p>",
            "<p>CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Displacement Reaction</p>",
            "<p>A + BC → AC + B</p>",
            "<p>Zn + CuSO₄ → ZnSO₄ + Cu</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Double Displacement Reaction</p>",
            "<p>AB + CD → AD + CB</p>",
            "<p>AgNO₃ + NaCl → AgCl + NaNO₃</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "qmyklys",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Oxidation and Reduction"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ik4n5db",
      "content": "Oxidation: Addition of oxygen or loss of hydrogen."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Exothermic Reactions",
        "Endothermic Reactions"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Heat Transfer",
          "Heat energy is released to surroundings",
          "Heat energy is absorbed from surroundings"
        ],
        [
          "Temperature Change",
          "Temperature of reaction system rises",
          "Temperature of reaction system drops"
        ],
        [
          "Common Examples",
          "Respiration, Combustion of fuels",
          "Photosynthesis, Thermal decomposition"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "zolg2a1"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hgnfywe",
      "content": "Reduction: Removal of oxygen or gain of hydrogen."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f0xrmcm",
      "content": "These reactions together are called redox reactions."
    },
    {
      "id": "mmsvk7u",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Exothermic and Endothermic Reactions"
    },
    {
      "id": "782or4e",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Reaction Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Explanation</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Exothermic Reaction</p>",
            "<p>Releases heat energy</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Endothermic Reaction</p>",
            "<p>Absorbs heat energy</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "vwtaxxi",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. Corrosion"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "akfxmhe",
      "content": "Corrosion is the gradual destruction of metals due to chemical reactions with the environment. Rusting of iron is the most common example."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "drcimbn",
      "content": "Example of rusting:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "onv74gz",
      "content": "Iron + Oxygen + Water → Rust"
    },
    {
      "id": "cjuizx3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "9. Rancidity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "68b91yu",
      "content": "Rancidity refers to the oxidation of fats and oils in food which causes unpleasant smell and taste. It can be prevented by storing food in airtight containers."
    },
    {
      "id": "jk1vxjs",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "10. Catalysts"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Identifying oxidizing vs. reducing agents in a given redox equation is a standard high-yield exam question.",
        "Balancing chemical equations strictly adheres to the Law of Conservation of Mass.",
        "Methods of preventing corrosion (such as galvanization) and rancidity (such as flushing with nitrogen gas) are regularly tested."
      ],
      "id": "8j75c42"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bjksnio",
      "content": "A catalyst is a substance that increases the rate of a chemical reaction without being consumed in the reaction."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b6kactk",
      "content": "Examples: Platinum, Iron catalyst in Haber process"
    },
    {
      "id": "stjbrdt",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Important Facts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0lepzzp",
      "content": "• Chemical equations represent chemical reactions."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vsszmlr",
      "content": "• Rusting of iron is a common example of corrosion."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "203byll",
      "content": "• Redox reactions involve oxidation and reduction."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mac7ah5",
      "content": "• Exothermic reactions release heat energy."
    }
  ],
  "enriched": true
}